we often hear the phrase  eat your rainbow and I'm a big advocate  on that what does it actually mean and  why is it important it is a simple way  of reminding us to consume a variety of  fruits and vegetables to get the  vitamins and minerals your body needs  the standard Western diet tends to fall  into the beige color just think of a  typical day porridge cereal granola  sausages pretzel sandwich pasta chicken  yeah you know what I mean  eating your rainbow means adding red  green yellow or orange purple blue and  white or brown fruits and vegetables  into your diet every single day each  color carries its own set of unique  healing properties which is called  phytochemicals it is these  phytochemicals that give these fruits  and vegetables its vibrant color and  healing properties we all know that we  should be consuming over five a day at  least three cups of vegetables and two  cups of fruits unfortunately most of us  only consume 1.8 cups a day eating a  rainbow is simple and it is actually the  first step you should take to get  healthy let's take a look at each color  red think of tomatoes strawberries  watermelon apples red peppers goji  berries cherries rice asuka beans red  quinoa and more red fruits and  vegetables are colored by a natural  plant pigment called lycopene lycopene  is a powerful antioxidant that can help  to keep your heart healthy and reduce  the risk of cancer especially prostate  cancer in a study of 50,000 men it was  found that men with the highest level of  lycopene with 21 percent less likely to  develop prostate cancer  in comparison to those with low levels  it can also prevent damage from  pesticides on our body not all of us can  afford organic produce free of pesticide  on a regular basis therefore it is very  important to consume red vegetables  fruits let's take a look at the next  color yellow and orange I'm sure you  have heard of eating your carrots being  good for your eyesight but why is that  so yellow and orange fruits and  vegetables are high in beta-carotene  which is converted to vitamin A in our  body vitamin A is important for  maintaining eye health boost immunity  and for cell growth to other carotenoids  are also present in yellow orange fruits  lutein and zeaxanthin  this helps prevent age-related eye  degeneration which can lead to blindness  just think of squashes carrots pumpkins  oranges lemons sweet potatoes peppers  yellow zucchinis mangoes papayas  cantaloupe and more another really  simple tip is to add a squeeze of lemon  juice to your food to increase iron  absorption plus it will add flavour to  your meal green vegetables are the most  common color we consume they are  extremely low in calories hence they can  help you to lose weight and to maintain  a healthy body weight plus they are  extremely high in fiber which means you  will feel fuller for longer green  vegetables have an abundance of vitamin  A C and K vitamin K is important to  protect your bones from osteoporosis  leafy greens like spinach and broccoli  are also rich in folate which is a B  vitamin especially important for  pregnant women to prevent birth defects  so besides consuming spinach and  broccoli other greens include bok choy  kill lettuce green grapes and apples  asparagus avocado kiwi fruit peas celery  and more let's move on to the next color  blue and purple unfortunately this food  group only makes up about 3% of the  average Western diet which tells us we  need to start increasing our intake in  these fruits and vegetables start to  consume more Bitteroot purple sweet  potato red cabbage eggplant blackberries  blueberries plums prunes  and purple grapes the plant pigment  anthocyanin is what gives blue or pulp  of fruits and vegetables they're  distinct unique color there is also some  anthocyanin in red fruits and vegetables  although it is not as present in  comparison to blue or purple fruits and  vegetables anthocyanin has antioxidant  property that protects cells from damage  and can have reduced the risk of cancer  stroke and heart disease anthocyanin  also promotes longevity here this wine  is high in anthocyanins rest virtual and  other flavonoids hence consuming wine in  small amounts can be good for your  health  flavonoids are also found in purple  sweet potato and it is one of the  reasons why the Japanese in Okinawa are  known for living a long and healthy life  and the final group is white and brown  white or brown vegetables are perhaps  the most neglected group of vegetables  although not technically a color of the  rainbow  what vegetables are rich in anthocyanins  a type of flavonoids and those and  things have been found to reduce blood  pressure in a ten-year study researchers  found that those who consumed white  fruits and vegetables regularly were 50%  less likely to get a stroke another  important element white and brown fruits  have is sulfur sulfur is important for  carotene which gives us hair and skin  their color a lack of sulfur will lead  to dull and saggy skin some white  vegetables such as garlic contains  allicin which has antimicrobial and anti  virus properties others such as bananas  and potatoes are a good source of  potassium so start adding cauliflower  mushroom garlic onions ginger bananas  parsnip turnips chickpeas and radish  into your diet the next time you shop  and cook think of the rainbow chart aim  to pack as many colors as you can into  your meals to start off consume at least  2 to 3 different colors of vegetables  and fruits in each meal and slowly  increase that  make your plate colorful and trust me  you will see the difference on your  waistline your health your body and also  your skin another tip is to create your  own rainbow smoothie by packing in all  the colors for example a blend of  strawberries mangoes spinach beetroot  and banana so guys it's your rainbow  every single day ok be sure to give this  video a thumbs up like this video share  this video on with all your friends and  hit the subscribe button for a lot more  fitness tips and nutritional advice  workout videos and colorful rainbow  recipes all the best
 

